IMPORT prelude

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 (24 & 25 Vict. c. 100) -- LAYER 1 (the law,
-- objectively): THE NOUNS.
--
-- Text encoded: the revised text on legislation.gov.uk at the point in time
-- 2026-04-29, as deposited in ukpga-Vict-24-25-100.txt (a plain-text rendering by a
-- converter script; it is not the official text and it is not marked up).
--
-- This module holds nouns only: the kinds of punishment the text names, the words the
-- text uses to classify an offence, the Act's offences as a list, and the one result
-- type that says "the Act does not answer".  It has no rules and no #EVAL.  Every
-- other module imports it.
--
-- WHAT THE TEXT MAKES THE PUNISHMENTS LOOK LIKE.  Every penalty clause in the text but
-- one carries ". . ." marks where words have been repealed or omitted.  The encoding
-- records what the words left show and never guesses what the dots hide.  Where a
-- punishment is named but its term is not stated, the term is the explicit constructor
-- `for a term the text does not state`, never 0 and never a borrowed figure.

§ `The nouns`

-- A term of years or months, "for life", or a term the text does not state.
-- "months" is used for every fixed term so that 3 months, 2 years and 14 years sit in
-- one unit (2 years = 24 months, 7 years = 84, 10 years = 120, 14 years = 168).
DECLARE Term IS ONE OF
    `for life`
    `for a term not exceeding` HAS `months` IS A NUMBER
    `for a term the text does not state`

-- The punishments the text names.  "Penal servitude" is the text's own word for a
-- custodial punishment (the Act does not define it); "imprisonment" is the text's own
-- word; the fine in section 42 is capped "together with costs", so the ceiling is the
-- sum of the two.
DECLARE Sentence IS ONE OF
    `penal servitude` HAS `term` IS A Term
    `imprisonment` HAS `term` IS A Term
    `imprisonment in default of paying a fine and costs` HAS `term` IS A Term
    `a fine, with any costs, not exceeding` HAS `pounds` IS A NUMBER

-- What the text calls an offence: "felony", "misdemeanor" (the text's spelling), "an
-- offence" (section 16 says only that), or nothing at all.
DECLARE Classification IS ONE OF
    felony
    misdemeanor
    `an offence, with no further classification`
    `no classification stated`

-- How the text says an offence is tried, where it says: "on indictment" (sections 16
-- and 47), "before two justices of the peace" (sections 39, 40 and 42), or not said.
DECLARE `Mode of trial` IS ONE OF
    `on indictment`
    `summarily before justices of the peace`
    `the text does not say`

-- Every offence-creating or penalty-stating provision of the text, one member each.
-- Section 25 (alternative verdict), sections 44 and 45 (certificate and bar), 65
-- (search warrant), 68 (Admiralty), 76 (procedure) and 78 (Scotland) are not offences
-- and are encoded in their own modules.  Section 5 is listed because it states the
-- penalty for manslaughter, although the Act does not define manslaughter.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `s 4: soliciting or proposing a murder`
    `s 5: manslaughter (the penalty only)`
    `s 16: threats to kill`
    `s 17: impeding a person trying to save their life from shipwreck`
    `s 18: wounding or causing grievous bodily harm, with intent`
    `s 20: wounding or inflicting grievous bodily harm`
    `s 21: attempting to choke or render insensible, to commit an indictable offence`
    `s 22: administering a stupefying drug, to commit an indictable offence`
    `s 23: administering poison so as to endanger life or inflict grievous bodily harm`
    `s 24: administering poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy`
    `s 26: neglecting or harming an apprentice or servant`
    `s 27: abandoning or exposing a child under two years`
    `s 28: causing bodily injury by an explosion`
    `s 29: causing an explosion, or sending or applying a dangerous thing, with intent`
    `s 30: placing an explosive near a building, ship or vessel, with intent`
    `s 31: setting a spring gun or man trap`
    `s 32: placing an obstruction on a railway, with intent to endanger`
    `s 33: casting a thing at a railway carriage, with intent`
    `s 34: endangering railway passengers`
    `s 35: bodily harm by furious driving or wilful misconduct`
    `s 36: obstructing or assaulting a clergyman or other minister`
    `s 37: assaulting a person preserving a vessel or wreck`
    `s 38: assault with intent to resist or prevent lawful apprehension`
    `s 39: violence to obstruct the sale or conveyance of grain`
    `s 40: violence to hinder a seaman, keelman or caster`
    `s 42: common assault or battery`
    `s 47: assault occasioning actual bodily harm`
    `s 57: bigamy`
    `s 58: administering drugs or using instruments to procure abortion`
    `s 59: supplying drugs or instruments to procure abortion`
    `s 60: concealing the birth of a child`
    `s 64: making or having gunpowder or a dangerous thing to commit a felony of this Act`

-- The one result type for a question the text does not decide.  "The Act does not say"
-- is an answer the encoder gives, not the law's own: it is returned where a provision
-- the question would need is blank or absent in the deposited text.
DECLARE `What the Act says` IS ONE OF
    `the Act makes it so`
    `the Act does not make it so`
    `the deposited text does not say`
